'''Josef Arne Gerdt Furumark''' (26 September 1903 – 8 October 1982) was a Swedish{{Sfn|Accorinti|2014|loc=p. 323, n. 3}} archaeologist who specialised in the ceramics of [[Mycenaean Greece]]. His three-volume work ''The Mycenaean Pottery'' established a categorisation and chronology of the ceramics of the [[Late Bronze Age]] on the Greek mainland. A 1943 review credited him with doing "for [[Mycenaean pottery]] what [[Humfry Payne|Payne]] did for Corinthian and [[John Beazley|Beazley]] for Attic wares",{{Sfn|Daniel|1943|p=252}} and subsequent scholars have considered the work a standard reference, though it received criticism for an over-rigid approach and for prioritising stylistic analysis at the expense of information about objects' archaeological contexts.

Furumark was born in the Norwegian capital of [[Oslo|Kristiania]] (now Oslo).{{Efn|name=Union}} After an unhappy childhood, in which he initially seemed set to follow his father into the paper business, he entered [[Uppsala University]] in Sweden in 1925 to study the humanities, and became a pupil of the archaeologist [[Axel W. Persson]]. He obtained his doctorate, with a thesis on the decoration of Mycenaean ceramics, in 1939, and became an associate professor at Uppsala in the same year. He led excavations at [[Sinta, Cyprus|Sinda]] on Cyprus, the first Swedish archaeological work on the island since the [[Second World War]], between 1947 and 1948, and became a professor at Uppsala in 1952. He remained there until his retirement in 1970, and served a year's term as director of the [[Swedish Institute at Athens]], a research centre for classical archaeology, between 1956 and 1957. He also conducted excavations at [[San Giovenale]] in Italy between 1962 and 1963, and published on the Mycenaean settlement of the island of [[Rhodes]].

Although an early sceptic of the idea that the [[Linear B]] script was used to write Greek, Furumark corresponded with [[Michael Ventris]], who deciphered it in 1953, and was one of the first archaeologists to use his conclusion that its language was indeed Greek. Furumark's work on ceramics helped to establish the distinctive nature of mainland Greek ("Helladic") ceramics in relation to Cretan ("[[Minoan civilization|Minoan]]") wares, and highlighted the ways in which both cultural groups influenced each other to varying extents over time. In doing so, he helped to overturn the narrative of [[Arthur Evans]] that Minoan Crete had dominated mainland Greece throughout the Late Bronze Age. He also established continuity between Mycenaean pottery and that of the following [[Protogeometric style|Protogeometric period]], and thereby that narratives which argued for the complete overturning of Greek culture at the end of the Bronze Age, such as that of the [[Dorian invasion]], were incorrect. 

==Biography==
Josef Arne Gerdt Furumark was born on 26 September 1903,{{sfn|Medwid|2000|p=112}} in the Norwegian capital of [[Oslo|Kristiania]] (now Oslo),{{Refn|{{cite web|url=https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?postid=Folk_128170567| publisher=National Archives of Sweden| title=Folkräkningar (Sveriges befolkning) 1930| lang=sv| trans-title=Census (Sweden's Population) 1930| access-date=20 July 2025}}}}{{Efn|Until 1905, Norway was [[Union between Sweden and Norway|in a personal union]] with Sweden.{{sfn|Douglas-Scott|2023|p=361}}|name=Union}} where his father worked as a businessman in the paper industry.{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=34}} He suffered from a skin condition throughout his life, which left him shy and socially isolated.{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=38}} Family life during his early years was unhappy; Furumark initially attended business school in Norway and worked in his father's offices in Oslo and in London, where at the age of nineteen he visited the [[British Museum]]. He later said that this visit encouraged him to change his studies to archaeology. In 1925, he entered Sweden's [[Uppsala University]], where he studied a wide [[humanities]] curriculum including classical archaeology, philosophy and theology. He eventually came to study there under the Mycenaean archaeologist [[Axel W. Persson]],{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=34}} under whom he took part in excavations in Greece.{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=35}} 

Furumark received his doctorate, with a dissertation on the subject of [[Mycenaean Greece|Mycenaean]] ceramic decoration, in 1939. In the same year, he was made an associate professor at Uppsala.{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=35}} In 1941, he published ''The Mycenaean Pottery: Analysis and Classification'' and ''The Chronology of Mycenaean Pottery,''{{Sfn|Hägg|1996|p=476}} a two-volume study which analysed, categorised and dated Late Bronze Age ceramics from the mainland of Greece according to their shapes and decorative motifs.{{Sfn|McDonald|Thomas|1990|p=300}} He had begun writing a third volume, ''Mycenaean Pottery: A History<sub>,</sub>'' by 1941, but abandoned the project by 1944.{{sfn|Siapkas|2018|p=14}} He published {{Lang|sv|Det äldsta Italien}}, a handbook of Italian archaeology, in 1947, which became widely cited: he planned, but never published, an edition in English.{{Sfn|Brunnsaker|1976|p=30}}

Between 1947 and 1948, Furumark excavated at [[Sinta, Cyprus|Sinda]] on Cyprus.{{Sfn|Hägg|1996|p=476}} Swedish archaeologists had excavated widely on the island before the [[Second World War]], but Furumark's was the first Swedish project there after 1945.{{Sfn|Styrenius|1994|pp=8–12}} The excavation was conducted in two campaigns; the first from 22 December 1947 to 22 January 1948, and the second from 25 March to 7 May 1948.{{Sfn|Furumark|2003|p=63}} [[Carl-Gustaf Styrenius]] later credited Furumark's excavations with expanding scholarly knowledge of migration from Greece to Cyprus at the end of the Bronze Age.{{Sfn|Styrenius|1994|p=12}} The project uncovered a [[Late Cypriot]] ({{Circa|1300|1200 BCE}}) settlement with [[Cyclopean masonry|Cyclopean walls]],{{Sfn|Göransson|2012|p=419}} though only excavated a small fraction of its total area of around {{Convert|46500|m2|acre}}.{{Sfn|Iacovou|2007|p=10}} In 1948, during a visit to the Bronze Age site of [[Hala Sultan Tekke (archaeological site)|Hala Sultan Tekke]] on the island, he noticed a large [[pithos]] in a field, which prompted new excavations around it.{{Sfn|Åström|1978|p=232}}

In 1950, Furumark argued, on the basis of the ceramics of Minoan Crete, that the idea of the Cretan site of [[Knossos]] being ruled by Greek-speakers in the Late&nbsp;Minoan II period ({{Circa|1490|1430 BCE}}) was "untenable":{{Refn|For the dates, see {{harvnb|Shelmerdine|2008|p=5}}.}} the fact of mainland rule over Crete in this period was established two years later by [[Decipherment of Linear B|the discovery]] by [[Michael Ventris]] that the [[Linear B]] tablets made there at this time were written in a form of Greek.{{Sfn|McDonald|Thomas|1990|p=314}} Furumark had maintained a correspondence with Ventris since 1951;{{Sfn|Hedlund|2003|p=10}} after Ventris proposed his decipherment, Furumark worked with {{ill|Gudmund Björck|sv}}, the professor of Greek at Uppsala, to check its accuracy. The two wrote to Ventris in October 1952 to give their verdict that he was correct.{{Sfn|Hedlund|2003|p=11}}{{Efn|After Ventris's death in a car accident in 1956, Furumark wrote his obituary in the Swedish newspaper ''[[Dagens Nyheter]]'', in which he called his death "an infinitely painful loss [of a] genius [and] shy young man".{{sfn|Hedlund|2003|p=12}}}} Furumark was one of the first archaeologists to use his decipherment in his own work.{{refn|name=ArtHistDict|1={{cite web| url=https://arthistorians.info/furumarka/| title=Furumark, Arne| website=Dictionary of Art Historians| access-date=30 June 2025}}}}{{Efn|Furumark later suggested that the Linear B script may have been developed on the Greek mainland during the [[Shaft Grave period]] ({{circa|1675|1500 BCE}}).{{sfn|Mylonas|1956|p=277}}}} Also in 1950, he argued that Mycenaean settlers had arrived on the island of [[Rhodes]] around the turn of the Late&nbsp;Helladic IIB and IIIA periods (that is, {{Circa|1400 BCE}}), that they lived peacefully alongside the pre-existing population, who used [[Minoan pottery]], and were allowed to live in existing houses. Mario Benzi, in 1988, called this idea "astonishing", and suggested that the Mycenaean incomers more probably subjugated the inhabitants by force.{{Sfn|Benzi|1988|p=59}} 

Furumark became Professor of Classical Archaeology and Ancient History at [[Uppsala University]] in 1952,{{Sfnm|1a1=Hägg|1y=1996|1p=476|2a1=Medwid|2y=2000|2p=112}} succeeding Persson.{{Sfn|Åström|2003|p=7}} In the early years of his professorship, he was involved in reorganising the university's collection of antiquities,{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=35}} and created a small museum in two of the university's rooms.{{Sfn|Brunnsaker|1976|p=31}} He served as director of the [[Swedish Institute at Athens]], a research centre for classical archaeology, between 1956 and 1957.{{Sfn|Accorinti|2014|loc=p. 323, n. 3}} Among his students at Uppsala were [[Margareta Lindgren]], who worked on Linear B,{{Sfn|Nosch|Enegren|2017|p=xxviii}} and [[Gisela Walberg]], who extended his work on Mycenaean ceramics to those of [[Middle Minoan]] Crete,{{Sfn|Hood|1978|p=375}} as well as {{Interlanguage link|Sture Brunnsåker|sv}} and {{Interlanguage link|Carl Nylander|sv}}.{{Sfn|Brunnsaker|1976|p=31}} His own academic work during his professorship, in contrast to his previous studies of Mycenaean and Italian archaeology, focused on the ancient Aegean scripts.{{Sfn|Brunnsaker|1976|pp=30–31}} In 1956, he gave a lecture in Berlin in which he attempted to decipher the [[Linear A]] tablets uncovered at the site of [[Hagia Triada]] on Crete,{{Sfn|MacKendrick|1981|p=120}} using the methods applied by Ventris to Linear B,{{Sfn|Braidwood|Mason|1958|p=59}} and posited that Linear A had similarities with the Anatolian [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European language]] of [[Luwian language|Luwian]].{{Sfn|MacKendrick|1981|p=120}}{{Efn|A manuscript on Linear A and [[Minoan religion]] was unpublished at the time of his death.{{sfn|Nylander|1983|p=37}}}} He was appointed a member of the [[Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences]] in 1958.{{Sfn|Pohl|1958|p=412}} Between 1962 and 1963, he excavated at [[San Giovenale]] in Italy,{{Sfn|Hägg|1996|p=476}} continuing excavations begun there in the 1950s by the [[Swedish Institute in Rome]].{{Sfn|Karlsson|2006|p=22}} After recovering from a period of serious illness in the late 1960s,{{Sfn|Nylander|1983|p=35}} he retired from his professorship in 1970,{{Sfnm|1a1=Hägg|1y=1996|1p=476|2a1=Medwid|2y=2000|2p=112}} and was succeeded by his former student Sture Brunnsåker.{{Sfn|Brunnsaker|1976|p=31}} He died on 8&nbsp;October 1982 in Uppsala.{{sfn|Medwid|2000|p=112}}

==Contributions to archaeology==
[[File:Cup squid 1300-1200 BC Staatliche Antikensammlungen.jpg|alt=Goblet-shaped ceramic vessel, with a tall stem and two handles, painted in black with a squid design|thumb|Mycenaean [[kylix]] of the Late Helladic IIIB period]]Johannes Siapkas, in 2022, identified Furumark as part of a Swedish tradition, traceable back to the work of {{ill|Erik Hedén|sv}} at the turn of the twentieth century, to distinguish the academic treatment of the ancient world from the popular understanding of it, and to prioritise scientific objectivity in its reconstruction.{{sfn|Siapkas|2022|p=114}} A 1943 review of ''The Mycenaean Pottery'' credited him with doing "for Mycenaean pottery what [[Humfry Payne|Payne]] did for Corinthian and [[John Beazley|Beazley]] for Attic wares";{{Sfn|Daniel|1943|p=252}} Siapkas compared his work with that of [[Johann Joachim Winckelmann]] on ancient sculpture.{{sfn|Siapkas|2018|p=7}}

''The Mycenaean Pottery'' was the first complete survey of its subject published in book form,{{Sfn|McDonald|Thomas|1990|p=300}} and became the established point of departure for subsequent studies.{{sfn|Siapkas|2018|p=7}} Furumark's approach developed from that of German stylistic analysis, known as {{Lang|de|Strukturforschung}} ({{Gloss|Structural Research}}), first practised in the 1920s by archaeologists such as {{ill|Friedrich Matz (born 1890)|de|Friedrich Matz der Jüngere|lt=Friedrich Matz}}.{{Efn|According to Furumark's student and biographer, Carl Nylander, he used to say that his only two teachers were Matz and the philosopher [[Axel Hägerström]].{{sfn|Nylander|1983|p=35}} Matz praised ''The Mycenaean Pottery'', writing that "Swedish archaeology, which has recently flourished, can be proud of an achievement such as this".{{sfn|Matz|1943|p=242}}}} However, Furumark rejected the association made in {{Lang|de|Strukturforschung}}, under the influence of [[Nazi racial theories]], between cultural styles and biological races.{{sfn|Siapkas|2018|p=15}} As well as ceramic vessels, he named and categorised the terracotta figurines known as [[Psi, phi and tau type figurine|psi, phi, and tau types]].{{sfn|French|1971|p=103}} The archaeologist {{ill|Robin Hägg|sv|Robin Hägg (arkeolog)|lt=Robin Hägg}} described the books as Furumark's "{{lang|la|magnum opus}} ... [and] ever since the standard work on this topic";{{Sfn|Hägg|1996|p=476}} Linda Medwid called them "the definitive reference on the subject" in 2000.{{Sfn|Medwid|2000|p=113}} Although the [[Absolute dating|absolute dates]] of Furumark's chronology have generally been revised, the categories and reference he assigned to particular shapes and motifs remain conventional vocabulary within the field.{{Sfn|Sherratt|2011|p=258}}

Furumark's contemporary [[Alan Wace]] criticised him for what he called "excessive [[pigeonholing]]" of ceramic styles, and for ignoring information about the archaeological contexts in which finds were unearthed; the latter criticism was also articulated by Wace's collaborator [[Carl Blegen]].{{sfn|Siapkas|2018|p=7}} [[Susan Sherratt]], in 2021, criticised Furumark's approach as "essentially stylistically based and unrealistically over-analytical", particularly by contrast with that of [[Elizabeth French|Lisa French]], who worked on Mycenaean pottery in the second half of the twentieth century.{{Sfn|Sherratt|2021}} [[Gerald Cadogan (archaeologist)|Gerald Cadogan]] described Furumark's approach as {{Lang|fr|[[dirigiste]]}} for what he considered its excessive devotion to ordered categorisation.{{Sfn|Cadogan|2003|p=353}}{{Efn|According to Jan Bouzek, who met Furumark, the latter intended his scheme to be used as a guideline rather than as a "fixed grid"; Bouzek criticised "the dogmatic interpretation of stylistic guidelines by less inspired followers [which] often turns a wise theory into a caricature".{{sfn|Bouzek|1994|p=218}}}} Sherratt elsewhere criticised Furumark's system for lumping together variations of shape and motif into single categories, on the basis of what he considered to be evolutionary relationships between them.{{Sfn|Sherratt|2011|p=258}} She also disagreed with his treatment of individual motifs and shapes in isolation, rather than as part of coherent decorative schemes.{{Sfn|Sherratt|2011|p=259}}

=== Influence on Minoan and Mycenaean studies ===
